<exercisegroup xml:id="exercisegroup-orthog-proj-geometry" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Properties of orthogonal projection, geometrically</title>

<introduction><p>
	For each of the following,
	begin by making a <em>fresh</em> copy of the orthogonal projection diagram in <xref ref="figure-orthog-terminology-proj" />,
	and then draw in additional vectors as instructed.
</p></introduction>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-orthog-proj-geometry-of-neg">
	<title>Projection of a negative vector</title>

	<task>

		<statement><p> Draw in vectors <m> - \uvec{u} </m> and <m> \proj_{\uvec{a}} (- \uvec{u}) </m>. </p></statement>

		<answer><image width="71.4%">
			<latex-image label="image-orthog-exercises-proj-geometry-of-neg">
				<xi:include href="proj-geometry.d/of-neg.tex" parse="text" />
			</latex-image>
			<shortdescription>
				Diagram illustrating the relationship between the orthogonal projection of a first vector onto a second vector
				and the orthogonal projection of the negative of the first vector onto the second.
			</shortdescription>
			<description>

				<p>
				A diagram consisting of two right triangles sharing a vertex at a point labelled <m> \zerovec </m>.
				A vector labelled <m> \uvec{a} </m> emanates rightwards from <m> \zerovec </m> at a slight positive slope.
				A second vector labelled <m> \uvec{u} </m> also emanates rightwards from <m> \zerovec </m>,
				at a greater positive slope and longer than <m> \uvec{a} </m>.
				A third vector labelled <m> \uproj{u}{a} </m> emanates rightwards from <m> \zerovec </m>,
				parallel to but longer than <m> \uvec{a} </m>,
				and a fourth unlabelled vector runs between the terminal points of <m> \uproj{u}{a} </m> and <m> \uvec{u} </m> so that a right-angled triangle is formed,
				with <m> \uvec{u} </m> as the hypotenuse.
				</p>

				<p>
				On the other side of <m> \zerovec </m>,
				the negative vector <m> - \uvec{u} </m> is drawn with initial point at <m> zerovec </m>.
				A vector labelled <m> \proj_{\uvec{a}} (-\uvec{u}) </m> emanates leftwards from <m> \zerovec </m>,
				parallel to but oppositely directed from <m> \uvec{a} </m>,
				and another unlabelled vector runs between the terminal points of <m> \proj_{\uvec{a}} (- \uvec{u}) </m> and <m> - \uvec{u} </m> so that a second right-angled triangle is formed,
				with <m> - \uvec{u} </m> as the hypotenuse.
				</p>

			</description>
		</image></answer>

	</task>

	<task>

		<statement><p>
			Based on your diagram and Euclidean geometry,
			give a geometric argument why <m> \proj_{\uvec{a}} (- \uvec{u}) </m> is equal to the negative of <m> \uproj{u}{a} </m>.
		</p></statement>

		<answer>

			<p>
			First,
			<m> \uproj{u}{a} </m> and <m> \proj_{\uvec{a}} (- \uvec{u}) </m> share the same initial point and their terminal points must both lie on the line through <m> \zerovec </m> that is parallel to <m> \uvec{a} </m>.
			So the two angles at <m> \zerovec </m> are opposite and therefore congruent,
			which makes the two right-angled triangles similar by Angle-Angle.
			But <m> \norm{- \uvec{u}} = \unorm{u} </m>,
			hence the two triangles must in fact be congruent.
			Therefore,
			<m> \proj_{\uvec{a}} (- \uvec{u}) </m> has the same length as <m> \uproj{u}{a} </m>.
			</p>

			<p>
			Furthermore, since the terminal points of <m> \uproj{u}{a} </m> and <m> \proj_{\uvec{a}} (- \uvec{u}) </m> are collinear,
			these two vectors are parallel but oppositely directed.
			Since they have the same length,
			we may conclude that they are negatives of each other.
			</p>

		</answer>

	</task>

	<task><p>
		Using our <xref ref="definition-orthog-projection" text="custom">formula for orthogonal projection</xref>,
		algebraically verify the identity <md> \proj_{\uvec{a}} (- \uvec{u}) = - \uproj{u}{a} </md>.
	</p></task>

</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-orthog-proj-geometry-onto-neg">
	<title>Projection onto a negative vector</title>

	<task>

		<statement><p> Draw in vectors <m> - \uvec{a} </m> and <m> \proj_{(- \uvec{a})} \uvec{u} </m>. </p></statement>

		<answer><image width="72%">
			<latex-image label="image-orthog-exercises-proj-geometry-onto-neg">
				<xi:include href="proj-geometry.d/onto-neg.tex" parse="text" />
			</latex-image>
			<shortdescription>
				Diagram illustrating the relationship between the orthogonal projection of a first vector onto a second vector
				and the orthogonal projection of the first vector onto the negative of the second.
			</shortdescription>
			<description>

				<p>
				A diagram consisting of a right triangle with one leg sitting on a line.
				A point representing the origin is plotted and labelled <m> \zerovec </m>.
				A vector labelled <m> \uvec{a} </m> emanates rightwards from <m> \zerovec </m> at a slight positive slope,
				and the line through <m> \zerovec </m> and parallel to <m> \uvec{a} </m> is sketched as a dashed line.
				A second vector labelled <m> \uvec{u} </m> also emanates rightwards from <m> \zerovec </m>,
				at a greater positive slope and longer than <m> \uvec{a} </m>.
				A third vector labelled <m> \uproj{u}{a} </m> emanates rightwards from <m> \zerovec </m>,
				parallel to but longer than <m> \uvec{a} </m>,
				and a fourth unlabelled vector runs between the terminal points of <m> \uproj{u}{a} </m> and <m> \uvec{u} </m> so that a right-angled triangle is formed,
				with <m> \uvec{u} </m> as the hypotenuse.
				</p>

				<p>
				The negative vector <m> - \uvec{a} </m> is also drawn with its initial point at the origin,
				so that it is oppositely directed to <m> \uvec{a} </m> along the dashed line,
				and finally a vector labelled <m> \proj_{(- \uvec{a})} \uvec{u} </m> is drawn spanning the same segment as <m> \uproj{u}{a} </m>.
				</p>

			</description>
		</image></answer>

	</task>

	<task>

		<statement><p>
			Based on your diagram and our conception of <term>orthogonal projection</term> as providing the answer to
			<xref ref="question-orthog-concepts-proj-motivation" />
			(see <xref ref="subsection-orthog-concepts-proj" />),
			give a geometric argument why <m> \proj_{(- \uvec{a})} \uvec{u} </m> is the same as <m> \uproj{u}{a} </m>.
		</p></statement>

		<answer><p>
			When <m> \uvec{a} </m> and <m> \uvec{u} </m> are positioned with initial points at the origin,
			as in the diagram,
			<m> \uproj{u}{a} </m> is defined to be the vector
			(also with initial point at the origin)
			whose terminal point lies at the point closest to <m> \uvec{u} </m> on the line through the origin and parallel to <m> \uvec{a} </m>.
			As <m> - \uvec{a} </m> is parallel to <m> \uvec{a} </m>,
			the point closest to <m> \uvec{u} </m> on the line through the origin and parallel to <m> - \uvec{a} </m> is the same point again,
			since the two lines are the same line.
			Therefore,
			<m> \proj_{(- \uvec{a})} \uvec{u} </m> has the same initial and terminal points as <m> \uproj{u}{a} </m>,
			and so the two vectors are the same.
		</p></answer>

	</task>

	<task><p>
		Using our <xref ref="definition-orthog-projection" text="custom">formula for orthogonal projection</xref>,
		algebraically verify the identity <md> \proj_{(- \uvec{a})} \uvec{u} = \uproj{u}{a} </md>.
	</p></task>

</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-orthog-proj-geometry-of-scaled">
	<title>Projection of a scaled vector</title>

	<task>

		<statement><p> Draw in vectors <m> 2 \uvec{u} </m> and <m> \proj_{\uvec{a}} (2 \uvec{u}) </m>. </p></statement>

		<answer><image width="71.4%">
			<latex-image label="image-orthog-exercises-proj-geometry-of-double">
				<xi:include href="proj-geometry.d/of-double.tex" parse="text" />
			</latex-image>
			<shortdescription>
				Diagram illustrating the relationship between the orthogonal projection of a first vector onto a second vector
				and the orthogonal projection of double the first vector onto the second.
			</shortdescription>
			<description>

				<p>
				A diagram consisting of two right triangles sharing a vertex at a point labelled <m> \zerovec </m>.
				A vector labelled <m> \uvec{a} </m> emanates rightwards from <m> \zerovec </m> at a slight positive slope.
				A second vector labelled <m> \uvec{u} </m> also emanates rightwards from <m> \zerovec </m>,
				at a greater positive slope and longer than <m> \uvec{a} </m>.
				A third vector labelled <m> \uproj{u}{a} </m> emanates rightwards from <m> \zerovec </m>,
				parallel to but longer than <m> \uvec{a} </m>,
				and a fourth unlabelled vector runs between the terminal points of <m> \uproj{u}{a} </m> and <m> \uvec{u} </m> so that a right-angled triangle is formed,
				with <m> \uvec{u} </m> as the hypotenuse.
				</p>

				<p>
				The scaled vector <m> 2 \uvec{u} </m> is also drawn with initial point at <m> \uvec{u} </m>.
				A vector labelled <m> \proj_{\uvec{a}} {(2 \uvec{u})} </m> again emanates rightwards from <m> \zerovec </m>,
				parallel to but longer than <m> \uvec{a} </m>,
				and another unlabelled vector runs between the terminal points of <m> \proj_{\uvec{a}} {(2 \uvec{u})} </m> and <m> 2 \uvec{u} </m> so that a second right-angled triangle is formed,
				with <m> 2 \uvec{u} </m> as the hypotenuse.
				</p>

			</description>
		</image></answer>

	</task>

	<task>

		<statement><p>
			Based on your diagram and Euclidean geometry,
			give a geometric argument why <m> \proj_{\uvec{a}} (2 \uvec{u}) </m> is equal to <m> 2 \uproj{u}{a} </m>.
		</p></statement>

		<answer>

			<p>
			The two right triangles in the diagram share an acute angle at <m> \zerovec </m>,
			and so are similar by Angle-Angle.
			In similar triangles,
			lengths of corresponding sides are at a constant ratio.
			Therefore,
			since the ratio of lengths of the hypotenuses <m> 2 \uvec{u} </m>  and <m> \uvec{u} </m> is <m> 2 </m>,
			the ratio of lengths of the bases <m> \proj_{\uvec{a}} (2 \uvec{u}) </m> and <m> \uproj{u}{a} </m> is also <m> 2 </m>.
			Furthermore,
			each of those base vectors is parallel to <m> \uvec{a} </m>,
			hence they are parallel to and therefore scalar multiples of each other.
			Combining all this,
			we conclude that <m> \proj_{\uvec{a}} (2 \uvec{u}) </m> must be equal to <m> 2 \uproj{u}{a} </m>.
			</p>

		</answer>

	</task>

	<task><p>
		Using our <xref ref="definition-orthog-projection" text="custom">formula for orthogonal projection</xref>,
		algebraically verify that the following identity holds for all scalars <m> k </m>: <md> \proj_{\uvec{a}} (k \uvec{u}) = k \uproj{u}{a} </md>.
	</p></task>

</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-orthog-proj-geometry-onto-scaled">
	<title>Projection onto a scaled vector</title>

	<task>

		<statement><p> Draw in vectors <m> 2 \uvec{a} </m> and <m> \proj_{(2 \uvec{a})} \uvec{u} </m>. </p></statement>

		<answer><image width="59.1%">
			<latex-image label="image-orthog-exercises-proj-geometry-onto-double">
				<xi:include href="proj-geometry.d/onto-double.tex" parse="text" />
			</latex-image>
			<shortdescription>
				Diagram illustrating the relationship between the orthogonal projection of a first vector onto a second vector
				and the orthogonal projection of the first vector onto the double of the second.
			</shortdescription>
			<description>

				<p>
				A diagram consisting of a right triangle with one leg sitting on a line.
				A point representing the origin is plotted and labelled <m> \zerovec </m>.
				A vector labelled <m> \uvec{a} </m> emanates rightwards from <m> \zerovec </m> at a slight positive slope,
				and the line through <m> \zerovec </m> and parallel to <m> \uvec{a} </m> is sketched as a dashed line.
				A second vector labelled <m> \uvec{u} </m> also emanates rightwards from <m> \zerovec </m>,
				at a greater positive slope and longer than <m> \uvec{a} </m>.
				A third vector labelled <m> \uproj{u}{a} </m> emanates rightwards from <m> \zerovec </m>,
				parallel to but longer than <m> \uvec{a} </m>,
				and a fourth unlabelled vector runs between the terminal points of <m> \uproj{u}{a} </m> and <m> \uvec{u} </m> so that a right-angled triangle is formed,
				with <m> \uvec{u} </m> as the hypotenuse.
				</p>

				<p>
				The scaled vector <m> 2 \uvec{a} </m> is also drawn with its initial point at the origin,
				so that it extends past the terminal point of <m> \uvec{a} </m> along the dashed line,
				and finally a vector labelled <m> \proj_{(2 \uvec{a})} \uvec{u} </m> is drawn spanning the same segment as <m> \uproj{u}{a} </m>.
				</p>

			</description>
		</image></answer>

	</task>

	<task>

		<statement><p>
			Based on your diagram and our conception of <term>orthogonal projection</term> as providing the answer to
			<xref ref="question-orthog-concepts-proj-motivation" />
			(see <xref ref="subsection-orthog-concepts-proj" />),
			give a geometric argument why <m> \proj_{(2 \uvec{a})} \uvec{u} </m> is the same as <m> \uproj{u}{a} </m>.
		</p></statement>

		<answer><p>
			When <m> \uvec{a} </m> and <m> \uvec{u} </m> are positioned with initial points at the origin,
			as in the diagram,
			<m> \uproj{u}{a} </m> is defined to be the vector
			(also with initial point at the origin)
			whose terminal point lies at the point closest to <m> \uvec{u} </m> on the line through the origin and parallel to <m> \uvec{a} </m>.
			As <m> 2 \uvec{a} </m> is parallel to <m> \uvec{a} </m>,
			the point closest to <m> \uvec{u} </m> on the line through the origin and parallel to <m> 2 \uvec{a} </m> is the same point again,
			since the two lines are the same line.
			Therefore,
			<m> \proj_{(2 \uvec{a})} \uvec{u} </m> has the same initial and terminal points as <m> \uproj{u}{a} </m>,
			and so the two vectors are the same.
		</p></answer>

	</task>

	<task><p>
		Using our <xref ref="definition-orthog-projection" text="custom">formula for orthogonal projection</xref>,
		algebraically verify the identity <md> \proj_{(k \uvec{a})} \uvec{u} = \uproj{u}{a} </md> for every scalar <m> k </m>.
	</p></task>

</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-proj-geometry-orthog-component-eq-proj-R2">
	<title>Orthogonal component versus orthogonal projection in <m> \R^2 </m></title>
	<!-- TODO link to future exercises -->

	<introduction><p> <alert>Note.</alert> For this exercise, assume <m> n = 2 </m>. </p></introduction>

	<task>

		<statement><p>
			Draw in a vector <m> \uvec{b} </m> orthogonal to <m> \uvec{a} </m>,
			with its initial point at <m> \zerovec </m>.
			Then draw in <m> \uproj{u}{b} </m>.
		</p></statement>

		<answer><image width="57.6%">
			<latex-image label="image-orthog-exercises-proj-geometry-orthog-component-eq-proj-R2">
				<xi:include href="proj-geometry.d/orthog-component-eq-proj-R2.tex" parse="text" />
			</latex-image>
			<shortdescription>
				Diagram illustrating congruence between the component of a first vector orthogonal to a second vector,
				and the orthogonal projection of the first vector onto a third vector that is orthogonal to the second.
			</shortdescription>
			<description><p>
				A diagram consisting of a rectangle in the <m> xy </m>-plane.
				A point labelled <m> zerovec </m> appears in the lower left corner of the rectangle,
				and a vector labelled <m> \uvec{u} </m> emanates from this point to the upper right corner of the rectangle,
				forming a diagonal for the rectangle.
				A vector labelled <m> \uvec{a} </m> emanates rightwards from the <m> \zerovec </m> point at a slightly positive slope along the bottom side of the rectangle,
				but not all the way to the lower right corner.
				Another vector parallel to <m> \uvec{a} </m> and labelled as <m> \uproj{u}{a} </m> emanates from the <m> \zerovec </m> point,
				this time extending all the way to the lower right corner of the rectangle,
				thus forming the bottom side of the rectangle.
				A vector labelled <m> \uvec{u} - \uproj{u}{a} </m> extends from the lower right corner to the upper right corner of the rectangle,
				forming the right side of the rectangle.
				A vector labelled <m> \uvec{b} </m> emanates upwards from the <m> \zerovec </m> point at a right angle to <m> \uvec{a} </m>,
				along the left side of the rectangle,
				but not all the way to the upper left corner.
				Another vector parallel to <m> \uvec{b} </m> and labelled as <m> \uproj{u}{b} </m> emanates from the <m> \zerovec </m> point,
				this time extending all the way to the upper left corner of the rectangle,
				thus forming the left side of the rectangle.
				Finally, a dashed line between the terminal points of <m> \uproj{u}{b} </m> and <m> \uvec{u} </m> forms the upper side of the rectangle.
			</p></description>
		</image></answer>

	</task>

	<task>

		<statement><p>
			Based on your diagram and Euclidean geometry,
			give a geometric argument for why <m> \uproj{u}{b} </m> is equal to the
			<term><xref ref="definition-orthog-orthog-component" text="title" /></term>
			in <m> \R^2 </m>.
		</p></statement>

		<answer>

			<p>
			In the diagram we have a quadrilateral with vertices at <m> \zerovec </m>
			and the terminal points of <m> \uproj{u}{a} </m>, <m> \uvec{u} </m>, and <m> \uproj{u}{b} </m>.
			Because <m> \uvec{b} </m> is assumed to be orthogonal to <m> \uvec{a} </m>,
			we have a right angle at the vertex at <m> \zerovec </m>.
			And the nature of <term>orthogonal projections</term> ensures that we have right angles at the vertices at the terminal points of <m> \uproj{u}{a} </m> and <m> \uproj{u}{b} </m>.
			</p>

			<p>
			In Euclidean geometry,
			a quadrilateral with three right angles must be a rectangle,
			and opposite sides in a rectangle are always parallel and congruent.
			Therefore,
			<m> \uproj{u}{b} </m> is parallel to (but not oppositely directed to) and the same length as <m> \uvec{u} - \uproj{u}{a} </m>.
			Therefore, <m> \uproj{u}{b} </m> is equal to the vector component of <m> \uvec{u} </m> orthogonal to <m> \uvec{a} </m>.
			</p>

		</answer>

	</task>

	<task>

		<statement><p> Why does a similar argument not work in <m> \R^n </m> for <m> n \gt 2 </m>? </p></statement>

		<answer>

			<p>
			This geometric argument only works in <m> \R^2 </m> because in <m> \R^2 </m> every vector that is orthogonal to <m> \uvec{a} </m> is parallel to every other vector orthogonal to <m> \uvec{a} </m>,
			forcing the shape to be a rectangle.
			In <m> \R^3 </m>,
			for example,
			there are <m> 360 </m> degrees of directions that are orthogonal to <m> \uvec{a} </m>,
			and choosing an arbitrary <m> \uvec{b} </m> would most likely cause <m> \uproj{u}{b} </m> to not even be parallel to <m> \uvec{u} - \uproj{u}{a} </m>.
			</p>

			<p component="one-semester">
			However,
			in a further course in linear algebra you may learn about <term>orthogonal projections</term> in <term>inner product spaces</term> more generally,
			in which case you will discover an analogous pattern involving projection onto an <term>orthogonal complement</term>.
			</p>

			<p component="two-semester">
			However,
			in <xref ref="chapter-projection" />
			we will encounter an analogous pattern involving projection onto an <term>orthogonal complement</term>
			(see <xref ref="proposition-projection-exist-unique" />).
			</p>

		</answer>

	</task>

</exercise>

</exercisegroup>
